children.push(...body([
  "The Desk's version of Lissome started with Gladys's notes and ended, four days later, with an alpaca eating a houseplant that had survived four administrations.",
  "Warren and Dana drove out on the strength of nothing more than a good phone call and Priya's insistence that the flap fit a shape she'd been tracking for months \u2014 sightings clustering, then a livestream, then a strangely specific piece of viral footage that vanished from every platform within hours of posting, faster than any takedown process Priya had ever timed. \u201cSomebody's cleaning up,\u201d she said. It was the least comforting sentence Dana had heard since Nowak's phone call.",
  "Lissome's water tower showed nothing to the naked eye and everything to Priya's filter: a hum in the ambient recordings from three separate phones the local sheriff's office had, with more competence than Warren expected, already collected as a courtesy to a videographer whose footage kept vanishing before she could show it to anyone official. The sheriff \u2014 a broad, unhurried woman named Cobb, forty years in a county that had never once needed her to run, with the particular patience of someone who had long ago made peace with never being believed about the interesting parts of her job \u2014 walked them through the file in a voice that never once suggested she found any of it as strange as Warren clearly did. She mentioned, almost in passing, that the family had also acquired an alpaca sometime during the reception, and that it had refused, categorically, to go anywhere near the best man, the groom's uncle, or \u2014 this got Warren's pen moving fast \u2014 the groom himself. She also mentioned a Mr. Huff, who had called the department eleven times since Saturday and was, in her professional opinion, not the problem this time, for once, a distinction she seemed to find almost disappointing.",
  "The Reyes place was a story-and-a-half on a street of identical ones, aluminum siding gone the soft chalk color every house on that block went after a decade of Indiana winters, a yard that smelled of cut grass and someone's charcoal grill three doors down. They found the alpaca in the backyard, wearing a bandanna a child had clearly put on it without permission, entirely unbothered by two federal employees and thoroughly opposed to standing within ten feet of Dominic Reyes, who came out to shake their hands, offered them lemonade with actual mint in it, and remembered both their names on the second try. Warren noted afterward that this was either good manners or a very good performance of good manners, and that the two had become, in his professional experience, increasingly hard to tell apart.",
  "Dana and Warren's cover for the visit, per the Desk's own standing script, was a federal environmental-exposure follow-up — a clipboard, a consent form, a request for a quick finger-prick — and it worked on Dominic exactly as well as it worked on everyone else in Lissome that week, which is to say completely, because nobody has ever once refused a man with a clipboard and a lanyard. The field kit was a cartridge about the size of a pregnancy test, and Priya, who had built the underlying assay from a Directorate paper she was not supposed to have access to, called it the Dipstick, a name that had survived three separate attempts by Colonel Buchanan to dignify it. It did not read DNA — DNA, the Desk would learn the expensive way, was no help at all. It read a chemical the body only seems to make when something has been quietly talking to the inside of a skull for thirty years. Dominic's cartridge turned a color the reference card did not have a friendly name for. Warren looked at it for a long moment and did not laugh. By dinnertime the alpaca had been loaded into Warren's sedan over Sheriff Cobb's mild objection and a family's enthusiastic one, folding itself into the back seat with the unbothered dignity of a creature who had clearly decided, at some point that afternoon, that federal transportation was simply the next scheduled item on its calendar, and had, without anyone officially deciding it, become the Desk's problem.",
  "Colonel Buchanan received the report the next morning with the particular stillness of a person deciding how many words to use in a memo she knew would be read three countries away. She used four. Escalate to Brussels immediately.",
]));

// Chapter V
children.push(...chapterHeading("V", "Two Missing Uploads"));
children.push(...guideQuote(
  "On podcasters: treat with caution. They are, technically, the only branch of the free press still doing the job, and they will not let anyone forget it.",
  "\u2014 The New Starter's Handbook, uncleared draft"
));
children.push(...body([
  "Jesse Tran found Nora because Jesse Tran found everything that vanished from the internet slightly too fast to be an accident; it was, more or less, his entire podcast, four hundred thousand subscribers who tuned in weekly to be told the world was stranger than the news admitted and safer than it sounded, delivered in that order so they could sleep afterward. He was thirty-one, wiry, dressed like a man who owned exactly four shirts and liked all of them, with a voice built for audio \u2014 warm, unhurried, the kind that made strangers want to keep talking long after they should have hung up \u2014 and a laugh that, Nora would later notice, arrived a half-second before he'd decided to let it.",
  "He had a mirrored copy of her eighty seconds, saved by a habit he'd built over six years of watching things disappear, and he had, more usefully to Nora, a working theory about who did the disappearing, built from three previous stories that had ended in the same quiet, professional silence: a gas leak that wasn't, a plane that circled an airport for forty extra minutes for reasons the tower never explained, a hospital that discharged four patients with the same rare complication on the same night and then closed those records to a records request with a speed that suggested somebody had been expecting the request before it was filed.",
  "Nora did not like him, and told him so within the first ninety seconds, which she would later describe as a personal best. She had spent a week being called a liar by strangers and a disappointment by her own sister, and the last thing her Tuesday required was a man sliding a recording app across the table like a business card. \u201cYou want the clip,\u201d she said. \u201cYou don't want to know what happened to me because of the clip.\u201d",
  "Jesse, who had built four hundred thousand subscribers on being underestimated in exactly that particular way, took longer than he should have to admit she wasn't wrong to be insulted, and spent the next twenty minutes being more defensive than persuasive, which did neither of them any favors. He used the word evidence. She used the word content. Neither landed the way either of them meant it to.",
  "\u201cIt's not the government exactly,\u201d he told her eventually, over coffee in a diner outside Lissome that smelled of fryer oil and burnt coffee in roughly equal measure, its vinyl booths patched with duct tape gone the same amber as the coffee itself, a bell over the door that hadn't rung true in a decade. Nora had chosen it because it still took cash and, she suspected, still kept its receipts on paper, and mostly because it put a table between them. \u201cIt's whoever the government calls when the government doesn't want to be the one who called.\u201d",
  "Nora told him about Dominic eventually too, mostly because she had run out of people who hadn't already decided she was lying or unwell. Jesse did not laugh, which she noted, and did not reach for his recorder, which she noted twice, and neither small mercy made her like him any better that afternoon. She agreed to keep talking to him the way you agree to share a lifeboat: not because you've warmed to the other passenger, but because the water doesn't much care whether you have.",
  "They got a different kind of confirmation on the way out of the diner, when a man neither of them recognized — forgettable in the specific, aggressive way of someone who has practiced being forgettable, mid-height, mid-weight, a windbreaker the color of every other windbreaker in that parking lot — fell into step beside Nora and said, pleasantly, that he'd enjoyed her footage before the internet decided it hadn't happened. He knew Dominic's name. He knew Mabs's. He knew, with a specificity that emptied Nora's stomach in one motion, which bathroom she had sat in for a long time on Thanksgiving. He did not threaten her, not in any sentence a lawyer could have used. He said only that people who noticed things sometimes stopped being useful once they'd noticed enough of them, and that he would rather she stayed useful a while longer, and then he wished her a good night, spoke perfectly ordinary, unaccented English right up to the last word, and got into a car that pulled away without hurrying.",
  "Jesse wanted to call it in immediately, to whom, he couldn't say, which was itself the problem. Nora wanted, more than she wanted to admit in front of a man she'd disliked at ninety seconds and still wasn't sure about at ninety minutes, to know how many other people that man had said good night to, and whether all of them had believed, the way she almost did, that he'd meant it kindly.",
  "They agreed on very little except the next step: find whoever had pulled her video, because whoever could make eighty seconds of wedding footage disappear from six platforms in under ten minutes, and then send a man to say good night to her in a parking lot, had resources neither a wedding videographer nor a podcaster possessed on their own, and resources, Jesse said, always led somewhere with a budget line, and a budget line always led somewhere with a building. It was not yet a partnership either of them would have called friendship. It would take a fence in Nevada, and worse than a fence, before either of them used that word out loud.",
  "Neither of them yet knew the building had a food court on top of it. They found that out later, the hard way, in a parking lot in Nevada, which is a sentence this book will get to.",
]));

/* ---------------- BOOK TWO ---------------- */
children.push(...bookHeading("BOOK TWO", "THE JOINT SKY DIRECTORATE", "The year Brussels finally answered its own phone"));

// Chapter VI
children.push(...chapterHeading("VI", "Brussels, Badly Lit"));
children.push(...guideQuote(
  "On committees: a committee that has met continuously since 1947 is not, whatever it tells itself, still deciding. It is simply meeting.",
  "\u2014 The New Starter's Handbook, uncleared draft"
));
children.push(...body([
  "The Joint Sky Directorate met in a building three blocks from NATO headquarters, a squat 1970s concrete block that had, on its directory, the word LOGISTICS and nothing else, which fooled precisely no one who had ever needed to deliver a package there and been asked for four forms of identification by a logistics company. The conference room smelled of instant coffee and the particular dust that settles on venetian blinds nobody has opened in a decade, and the overhead light had a flicker in its left third that three separate facilities requests had failed to fix, which was where the chapter's name came from.",
  "Director-General Ingrid Halvorsen \u2014 sixty, silver hair cut short and precise, a Norwegian vowel still audible under thirty years of English, a stillness in her hands that read, in a room full of fidgeters, as the most alarming thing about her \u2014 ran it the way she had run every room of her career: by being the only calm person in it and letting the calm do the persuading. Around the table sat the liaisons who made the Directorate what it actually was: less an organization than a truce \u2014 Agent Duval from the French service, silver-templed, a cigarette he never lit turning over and over between two fingers, who had brought a single folder and would share exactly what was written on its cover; Agent Whitcombe from the British service, tall and stooped and permanently rumpled in a way that suggested he'd once owned an iron and had made his peace with its loss, whose dry commentary functioned as the meeting's only reliable comic relief and whose actual reports were, everyone privately agreed, the most useful in the building; Agent Berthold from the German service, square-built, a voice that landed each syllable with the same even weight, who insisted on minutes in triplicate and had once delayed an actual crisis response by four minutes over a disputed translation of the word \u201cimminent\u201d; and Agent Cole from the American service, younger than the rest, sharp-suited, who wanted, above all else, a budget line she could defend to a committee that did not officially know the Directorate existed.",
  "Colonel Buchanan's escalation from the Indiana desk sat at the center of the table: a wedding video that would not stay deleted, an alpaca with, in Whitcombe's phrase, \u201capparently better clearance than several of the people in this room,\u201d and a wave of similar sightings that Priya Chen's pattern \u2014 now formally adopted, uncredited, as \u201cthe Chen index,\u201d a fact that would infuriate Priya for exactly as long as it took Halvorsen to fix it \u2014 placed on a curve that had not looked this steep since 1947.",
  "Agent Berthold, before Duval got that far, had already spent eleven minutes building a considerably more confident theory: that the wedding-video anomaly was, in fact, the visible signature of a Russian electromagnetic-pulse test being run under cover of a civilian celebration, a hypothesis he supported with a chart, three appendices, and a tone that made revising it seem like a personal insult to the chart. Whitcombe let him finish, entirely out of professional courtesy, before pointing out, gently, that Russia had not tested an alpaca since roughly the fall of the Soviet Union and was unlikely to start now over a wedding in Indiana. Berthold amended the theory rather than abandoning it, on the grounds that the alpaca could plausibly be a delivery mechanism, and was still drafting the revision when Halvorsen moved the meeting along.",
  "Agent Duval proposed, not entirely seriously, that the Directorate consider a formal overture: a translated greeting, offered in good faith, along the old model. Whitcombe reminded the table, with the weary patience of a man who had read the same file three times, that the last recorded attempt at exactly that \u2014 three men and a flag, walking toward a hatch in a field in the previous century \u2014 was remembered chiefly for how briefly it had lasted. Duval withdrew the suggestion. Nobody minuted whose idea it had originally been, which everybody at the table understood to be its own small mercy.",
  "There was, underneath the comedy, a harder current in the room that week, one Halvorsen did her best to keep off the record: certain capitals wanted a decisive result before Starfall, not another briefing paper. Decisive, in the vocabulary the Directorate's political masters preferred, rarely meant conversation, and Colonel Buchanan, alone at the table, did not think that vocabulary was wrong.",
  "\u201cWe have a private citizen,\u201d Halvorsen said, \u201ccircling this case with a podcast host and better raw footage than our own field office collected. We can recruit her, surveil her, or ignore her, and I would like it on the record that ignoring her is not actually one of the three options, whatever Agent Duval's face is currently suggesting.\u201d",
  "Duval's face suggested a great deal. Halvorsen moved on anyway, to the part of the briefing that mattered: whatever had first noticed Earth's radio chatter back in 1947 had waited three more decades before doing anything about it, and the thing it eventually did, forty-six years ago, was start growing a generation of infiltrators disguised as somebody else's children. The Directorate's own shorthand for the whole apparatus \u2014 the ship, the hybrids, the patient decades of paperwork behind both \u2014 had been, since before anyone currently in the room was cleared to ask why, the Feed. And Halo Systems \u2014 a satellite company that sold the world cheap orbital internet, and had, beneath that business, run the Directorate's only real operational arm for eleven years \u2014 had just built the Feed the biggest broadcast in human history to finish what it started, in the form of a global event called Starfall, eight days out.",
  "\u201cGet me Kade,\u201d Halvorsen finished, \u201cand get me the wedding videographer. In that order, because he'll actually answer the phone.\u201d",
]));

// Chapter VII
children.push(...chapterHeading("VII", "The Company That Owns the Sky"));
children.push(...guideQuote(
  "On cover stories: the best lie is a profitable one. A profitable lie audits itself.",
  "\u2014 The New Starter's Handbook, uncleared draft"
));
children.push(...body([
  "Halo Systems sold the world orbital internet from a campus that had been, in a previous decade, a suburban shopping mall, which meant its planetary defense apparatus ran beneath a food court a sentimental facilities director had refused to fully demolish; the escalators still worked, the fountain in the atrium still ran, and the whole building still carried, under the fresh paint, the specific echoing acoustics of a place built for teenagers to loiter in. Dana, summoned upstairs and then, disorientingly, downstairs again, was shown a lobby, a gift shop, and a livestream studio where a rotating cast of employees unboxed the company's consumer hardware for millions of weekly subscribers \u2014 a cover so profitable in its own right that Dana understood immediately why it had lasted eleven years without anyone official noticing.",
  "Below the food court, past a door marked EMPLOYEES and a second door marked nothing at all, a control room made the Desk's basement look like the joke everyone already knew it was: cold air, blue light, the low electrical hum of servers that never slept, and none of the microwaved-lunch smell that had followed Dana up from Indiana. Screens tracked orbital debris, weather, and a live global overlay of the exact hum Priya had spent months chasing, mapped in real time by people who had clearly been mapping it since long before the Desk existed.",
  "Elias Kade \u2014 mid-fifties, silver crew cut, the kind of permanent squint pilots and astronauts keep long after they've stopped needing to read an instrument panel in bright sun, a voice pitched low and level and built for delivering bad news to a room without raising it \u2014 introduced himself the way a man does who has stopped needing the introduction to do any work for him. He offered Dana a chair and the truth, in that order, which she was coming to recognize as the higher compliment in this line of work.",
  "\u201cWe used to tell new arrivals it came from somewhere far away,\u201d Kade said. \u201cIt's a better story. People like a war with a commute. The truth is worse for morale and better for physics.\u201d",
  "Kade unrolled a decades-old astronomical survey with a patch of sky near Jupiter circled in a hand that wasn't his. \u201cThey left home more than forty thousand years ago,\u201d he said. \u201cTheir sun was already going wrong, and nobody has ever built anything faster than light, so they built something that didn't need to be fast. It needed to hold a population instead of a crew. A generation ship. Everyone currently aboard was born aboard. Nobody currently making decisions has ever stood on the ground their species left.\u201d",
  "\u201cThey're still travelling,\u201d Dana guessed.",
  "\u201cThey stopped travelling a long time ago. They're parked, in Jupiter's shadow more or less, tucked in with a swarm of rock the surveys had already agreed was boring. Have been for longer than anyone downstairs will tell me exactly. A great deal longer than forty-six years, which is the number you actually want. It has a name, according to Feld \u2014 several committees long, something that translates roughly as Vessel of Continuance, Charter Revised \u2014 and everyone currently aboard it, Feld included, just calls it the Boat. Jupiter, for its part, has spent four and a half billion years being enormous and has never once been asked to notice anything, and did not start now.\u201d",
  "\u201cWhat happened forty-six years ago?\u201d",
  "\u201cThey came closer. Sent small craft in-system, quietly, and did the thing a patient species does once it's finally near enough to a living world to plan a visit: took samples. Blood, tissue, the ordinary theft people have been reporting to police stations for exactly that long without anyone official writing it down as anything but nerves. They'd been travelling so long that whatever they looked like when they left wasn't going to walk down an American street without somebody noticing. So they built a generation that would.\u201d",
  "Kade let that sit a moment before he finished it. \u201cThey grew hybrids. Old technique, older than ours, not especially mysterious once you're told it's possible \u2014 and swapped them, as infants, for children born the same night in the same hospitals. The originals went up to be studied. Some still are. The substitutes went home with people who never once doubted whose baby they were holding.\u201d",
  "The room tilted for Dana the way it had the morning the confetti came down. \u201cThey were raised human.\u201d",
  "\u201cRaised by humans. Not quite the same sentence. Every one of them got something installed at the base of the skull in the same hospital procedure that did the swapping, smaller than a grain of rice, doing two jobs at once ever since. Most of what it does is trivial and constant \u2014 a pulse, basically, the alien equivalent of a dog tag: alive, located, unharmed. It doesn't have the power budget to stream a life in real time, whatever the movies would have you believe. The actual content \u2014 what a person thinks, notices, suspects \u2014 only uploads during a visit. Missing hours a mother would blame on a bad babysitter, a fever, a fall nobody could quite remember happening. Spaced carefully enough, over enough years, to run a whole second curriculum alongside the one at the local school, and to empty out whatever the first curriculum had put in that quarter. You grow up going to birthday parties and Little League, and also, three or four times a year, somewhere with no windows and nothing on the walls that would remind you the planet under your feet had a name. Do that from infancy and you get someone who can pass a lie detector about loving their sister, because they do. And still answer a bell that was never rung by anyone the sister ever met.\u201d",
  "Kade let Dana sit with that before he added the part he clearly liked least. \u201cWhich means whatever your Desk has been doing to Dominic Reyes for the last several weeks is currently sitting in his head like a letter he hasn't mailed yet. Nobody up there knows a thing about you until his next visit is due.\u201d",
  "\u201cWhen's that,\u201d Dana said, already reaching for her phone.",
  "\u201cThat's the good news and the bad news in the same sentence, isn't it,\u201d Kade admitted. \u201cWe don't know. Feld's stopped getting visits, obviously. We're working from his old schedule and hoping the ship keeps a calendar the way everyone else does.\u201d",
  "\u201cThat's Dominic,\u201d Dana said. It wasn't a question.",
  "\u201cThat's most of what the Dipstick catches, once you know to look. Call it a second education rather than a possession \u2014 one running quietly underneath the first for thirty-odd years, waiting for a signal loud enough to hear.\u201d",
]));
children.push(...guideQuote(
  "On generation ships: a vessel built to carry a civilisation for forty thousand years will, as a matter of engineering necessity, also carry that civilisation's committees, subcommittees, and at least one working group whose original mandate nobody currently aboard can locate.",
  "\u2014 The New Starter's Handbook, uncleared draft"
));
children.push(...body([
  "\u201cWhy now,\u201d Dana pressed. \u201cWhy Starfall, why this year, after forty-six years of patience.\u201d",
  "\u201cTwo reasons, best as Feld can piece together, and neither one is conquest for its own sake. The ship is old \u2014 forty thousand years and however many generations of duct tape past its warranty \u2014 and something that size doesn't fail gracefully. And we keep building better eyes. Halo put four hundred satellites up this year alone, and the next constellation is good enough to notice something the size of a small moon sitting where a small moon has no business sitting. They're not choosing this moment because they're finally ready. They're choosing it because they're increasingly afraid we're about to notice them whether they're ready or not, and a species that's been patient for forty thousand years does not enjoy discovering it has a deadline.\u201d",
  "\u201cProxies,\u201d Kade said, before Dana could ask. \u201cNot adults who picked something up along the way. Hybrids, swapped in as infants, raised human right up until the bell rings. Most don't know what they are. A few have found out early, the way Feld did, and most of those decided the life they'd actually lived outweighed the one they'd been assigned. One of them decided it loudly enough to walk into a Directorate field office. He's most of the reason we know any of this, and I'd rather you heard the rest from him than from me.\u201d",
  "Kade told her about Starfall last, because it was the part he clearly hated saying out loud: his own company's global light show, timed to a total eclipse, one point one billion people watching a single synchronized broadcast, eight days out \u2014 loud enough, wide enough, and synchronized enough to reach every sleeping hybrid on the planet at once, and ring the bell for all of them on the same night.",
  "\u201cWe built the microphone,\u201d Kade said. \u201cThey've been waiting forty-six years for someone to hand them the stage. I keep asking myself whether the fight we've been having is even the right one, or whether nobody ever tried handing that ship anything else.\u201d",
]));

// Chapter VIII
children.push(...chapterHeading("VIII", "What Jesse Found in the Desert"));
children.push(...guideQuote(
  "On deserts: every country keeps at least one. This is not a coincidence. It is a filing system.",
  "\u2014 The New Starter's Handbook, uncleared draft"
));
children.push(...body([
  "The trail Jesse's instincts and Nora's stubbornness put together led, after five days and one very bad motel, to a fenced compound in a stretch of Nevada that didn't bother pretending to be scenic — flat scrub the color of a paper bag, a heat that sat on the skin like a hand, the smell of hot creosote and diesel from the generator shed carrying half a mile on a dead-still night — land that public records swore had closed in 2019 as a cryptocurrency mining operation and that Jesse's thermal-imaging contact swore was running hotter than anything abandoned has any business running.",
  "In the hour before the fence, in a motel room that smelled like the ice machine down the hall, Jesse caught himself laughing at something Nora said for the fourth time that night and made a private note, filed immediately under problems for later, that he no longer found her remotely insufferable. Nora, for her part, had stopped bracing for him to reach for his recorder some unnoticed number of days earlier, and couldn't have named the day it happened if asked. He'd almost told her so. He filed that for later too. Then the thermal-imaging contact texted back with coordinates, and the moment did what moments on a stakeout always do, which is wait its turn.",
  "They went at night, because Jesse's rules for this sort of thing were built on years of podcast research and, he admitted, several documentaries he probably shouldn't have taken so literally. The fence had been reinforced well after the facility supposedly closed. Nora, easing along it in the dark, put a hand flat on Jesse's chest and stopped him one step before a motion light would have found him; neither of them discussed, afterward, how naturally her hand had done that, or how long it stayed. The server racks inside, when they finally got a look through a gap Jesse insisted on calling \u201ca pre-existing opening\u201d for legal reasons neither of them examined too closely, weren't mining anything. They were listening \u2014 to the exact hum Nora's own footage had carried, audible, if you knew Priya's filter, which they did not, but which the racks' status lights blinked out in a rhythm too even to be cooling fans.",
  "What the racks were listening to, Priya would confirm days later, was not addressed to anyone on Earth at all. It was a relay, patched together and quietly maintained for four and a half decades, forwarding everything it heard out past the asteroid belt to something considerably larger than a server farm.",
  "They were three minutes from the fence when the actual professionals arrived: a Directorate field team, sent by Halvorsen the day before on a lead Priya had traced independently, who caught two civilians with a camera and a recorder crouched outside a facility neither civilian was supposed to know existed, and who spent a genuinely uncomfortable twenty minutes trying to determine whether Nora and Jesse were assets, liabilities, or simply the unluckiest true-crime podcast duo in America.",
  "It was Steve, riding along in the Directorate van over Colonel Buchanan's written objection because Priya refused to travel without him after Lissome, who settled the question, by declining \u2014 with total, unbudgeable, alpaca serenity \u2014 to exit the van anywhere near the field team's newest and most recently hired technical consultant, a man who had joined the operation eleven days earlier with a spotless r\u00e9sum\u00e9, no birth certificate that traced back any further than that r\u00e9sum\u00e9, and, once somebody finally thought to ask him for a Dipstick reading, blood the reference card did not have a friendly name for at all. Not a hybrid, Feld would confirm later, once he was shown the file. Actual crew. The ship kept a small, quiet rotation of them on the ground to run logistics the hybrids weren't trusted with, on the reasonable theory that a person raised entirely human might, eventually, get ideas.",
  "What they did with him afterward was not Nora and Jesse's business, and Buchanan made sure of it, but Dana would tell Nora later, in the flat voice of someone repeating something she hadn't enjoyed watching, that he stopped bothering to keep up the small talk within an hour of the cartridge turning the wrong color, and that the small talk, once Warren checked, turned out to have been assembled entirely out of other people's recorded interviews \u2014 a very good impression with nothing English behind it at all. Somebody, following a standard-intake checklist nobody had thought to update for this particular guest, offered him a sandwich. He held it, examined it with what a report later called clinical interest, ate approximately one bite, and was violently, comprehensively sick across a table two Directorate lawyers had specifically asked him not to be sick across. Warren's dry note in the file \u2014 subject's biology appears unable to tolerate Earth cuisine, in either direction \u2014 became, unofficially, the most quoted sentence in the whole operation. Priya spent the next three days feeding recordings of him into the same pattern-matching pipeline she'd built to find a missing satellite, with an AI translation layer bolted on by a contractor who billed by the hour and did not ask what the client was for, and got back, after three days, less a sentence than a mood: something that repeated, patiently, in whatever the software's closest available words were, unfinished, and continue, and, once, more plainly than the rest, not this way.",
  "Steve, for his part, chose that exact moment to lean his considerable weight against Jesse in a gesture Priya swore was affection and Jesse swore was an alpaca deciding, wordlessly, that whatever he'd been about to say to Nora in the motel could wait a little longer. It could. It did.",
  "Nora and Jesse were not arrested. They were, more disorientingly, thanked, debriefed, and offered coffee by a Colonel Buchanan who seemed personally affronted that two amateurs had found the facility a full day before her professionals had planned to raid it, and who used the word \u201cirregular\u201d four times in a sentence that otherwise sounded a great deal like an apology.",
  "\u201cYou should talk to Dana Osei,\u201d Buchanan said, in the tone of someone closing a file she intended to reopen the moment nobody was watching. \u201cShe's had your name in a report for two weeks and didn't know it.\u201d",
]));

// Chapter IX
children.push(...chapterHeading("IX", "Aboard"));
children.push(...guideQuote(
  "On hospitality: a civilisation that has not received visitors in forty thousand years will, on the rare occasion it takes any, prove unpracticed at nearly every part of the experience except the paperwork.",
  "\u2014 The New Starter's Handbook, uncleared draft"
));
children.push(...body([
  "They took Nora and Jesse two nights after the fence, which finally answered the question of what the man in the parking lot had meant by useful a while longer.",
  "It did not feel like an abduction from the inside, which Nora would later say was the most unsettling part of the whole night. There was no beam, no table, no cold light. There was a van ordinary enough that Jesse checked twice for a rental sticker, and a headache that started somewhere behind Nora's eyes and ended, with no sensation of travel in between, in a room the wrong shape for a room \u2014 curved where walls are supposed to be flat, in a way the eye kept trying and failing to file under corridor.",
  "The ship, up close, was not the gleaming thing forty thousand years of other people's television had prepared them for. It was old the way anything is old that has been patched more times than it was ever built: handrails worn pale by hands from three different generations of the same committee, an air recycler doing its unglamorous and slightly asthmatic best, corridors with the scuffed patience of a building that has never once been allowed to be replaced, only maintained. Somewhere, distantly, something that was almost certainly a public address system was almost certainly announcing a shift change.",
  "Their escort introduced himself, eventually, in perfectly unaccented English, as a hybrid raised outside Columbus, active four years, and \u2014 he said this part the way a man discloses a chronic condition \u2014 still undecided about the whole arrangement. He did not give a name that meant anything to write down. Nora, out of a habit she couldn't turn off even here, thought of him as the Escort and never learned better. He was, up close, entirely ordinary to look at \u2014 medium height, a runner's build, a voice pitched to be forgettable in a crowd, which she understood, by the end of the night, to be the entire design brief.",
  "Hospitality, on a ship that had not had visitors in forty thousand years, turned out to mean a bowl. What was in it had the color and approximate viscosity of a healing bruise, smelled distantly of wet cardboard and, underneath that, something Nora's brain insisted on filing next to low tide, and had been prepared, the Escort said with visible pride, according to a nutritional standard that had kept a closed population alive for two thousand generations without a single recorded case of scurvy. Jesse ate a spoonful out of a reporter's reflexive fear of being rude to a source. He did not finish it, and did not, for the rest of the night, entirely trust his own stomach's opinion of the matter. The Escort ate the rest of the bowl without comment, the way a person eats something they stopped tasting decades ago.",
  "The alienated humans were worse, and different, in a way that took Nora longer to understand than it should have, and they were not, she would insist afterward to anyone who assumed otherwise, all the same person wearing different faces. A heavyset man near the door had the specific coiled stillness of someone who volunteers for the difficult jobs; the one who lunged was younger, thinner, and lunged the way a man lunges when he has been waiting a long time for permission rather than an opportunity; a third, older, hung back near the wall and watched Nora's hands rather than her face, with something that was not quite friendliness but was not the flat, weather-reading blankness of the rest of them either. Jesse only had time to get an arm across Nora and a shoulder into the young one's path before Nora, a half-second later, got a fistful of the Escort's sleeve and hauled him bodily between all three of them, which turned out to be the thing that actually worked. Whatever the Escort said next, sharp and fast in a tongue Jesse's ear couldn't find a single cognate in, made the young one step back. It did not make him, or the older one by the wall, look any friendlier. The older one, Nora noticed, was the last to look away.",
  "\u201cThey don't get taught English,\u201d the Escort said, once they were past. \u201cWhy would they. Nobody ever planned on sending them down.\u201d",
  "\u201cThey're human,\u201d Nora said. It came out smaller than she meant it to.",
  "\u201cThey're crew. Third generation of it, some of them \u2014 the first ones taken are long dead, and the ones you just walked past were born up here to parents who were also born up here, to people who were taken as infants themselves. Human is a biology in that corridor, not a loyalty. You're the first actual Earth they've stood this close to in their lives, and I wouldn't recommend testing whether that makes anyone nostalgic.\u201d",
  "It emerged, on the walk that followed, that the ship's actual intelligence on Earth was both extensive and roughly forty years behind schedule, most of it gathered in one dense burst around the time the sampling program began and only patched since through whatever a returning hybrid happened to notice on a given visit. The hybrid curriculum still taught, with total institutional confidence, that the correct way to blend in at an American gas station was to pay with a personal cheque. It taught that disco was a going concern. It had a whole unit, delivered with a straight face by whoever delivered such things, on the etiquette of the rotary telephone. Nora asked, not entirely rhetorically, how anyone had managed to pass as a modern nine-year-old on that curriculum, and the Escort said, with the closest thing to genuine embarrassment she'd seen from him, that the honest answer was television, and that television had been doing quiet, uncredited work for this operation since roughly 1994.",
  "Nora asked what she had been trying not to ask since the corridor: what the ship had actually wanted with the real infants, forty-six years and longer of them, beyond the word studied, which had never once sounded to her like the whole sentence. The Escort didn't dress it up. \u201cPartly you can't leave a live baby behind without questions, and killing one turns out to be a harder policy to write down than people assume, even up here. But mostly, no. A population this size, sealed this long, breeds itself into a smaller and smaller room genetically whether it wants to or not. Forty thousand years is a long time to be your own only neighbours. They needed what your species keeps making by accident. Some of what you'd call the studying was a lab. Most of it, eventually, was a wedding.\u201d",
  "It also explained, though the Escort took visibly less pleasure in explaining this part, the handful of times over the decades that the ship had put one of its own Earth-born, Earth-raised, no-English-at-all descendants back down on the actual ground \u2014 never as policy, he said, more as a valve, when a council session ran hot enough that somebody needed to be told they could leave if they truly wanted to, and somebody occasionally took them up on it and regretted it within the hour. It never went well. A person with no language anyone above ground could place, no papers, and thirty years of ship instinct instead of manners does not blend into a rest stop. Jesse went very quiet, the way he did when a childhood memory turned out to have a citation. \u201cI've covered two of those,\u201d he said. \u201cA hitchhiker outside Elko who bit a state trooper and spoke what a linguist on the local news called \u2018nothing on record.\u2019 A woman found walking the shoulder of I-70 in January with no coat and no frostbite, who\u2019d been gone by the time anyone official arrived. I ran both as unsolved. I never once considered running them as the same story.\u201d",
  "Jesse asked the obvious, unforgivable, necessary question: whether the Escort trusted them more than his own \u2014 he groped for the word, and the Escort, not unkindly, supplied it \u2014 kind. The answer took longer arriving than the question had.",
  "\u201cI trust people who've had a choice put in front of them at some point,\u201d he said. \u201cThey never got one. I had a choice put in front of me before a man named Feld ever got his. Doesn't mean anything to you yet. It will. The council up here isn't wrong that the ship is dying and needs somewhere to land its people. It's wrong about the only two options it's seriously discussed in a hundred and forty years, which are take it, or wait politely to be allowed to. Nobody's put a third option on the agenda that survived a vote by someone whose grandmother still remembered the old sun.\u201d",
  "He showed them, because Nora asked and because he seemed, in some tired administrative way, glad someone finally had: a nursery, decommissioned decades back and kept, without much affection, as what he called a museum piece, where infants bound for Earth's hospitals had once had something installed at the base of the skull, smaller than a grain of rice, that did two jobs at once \u2014 a constant, low pulse that only ever said alive, located, unharmed, and a second channel that opened just a few times a year, during a visit, to empty a life into the ship and refill it with instructions, whether or not the person carrying it ever knew that counted as a conversation.",
  "\u201cDominic's been talking to this ship since he was four days old,\u201d the Escort added. \u201cHe just never knew it counted as talking.\u201d",
  "Nora didn't get to finish asking what happened to people like her, specifically, once a ship decided it no longer needed to listen politely. The Escort cut in before she'd finished forming it, the way a man answers something he's had to argue himself into first. \u201cYou're going back,\u201d he said. \u201cThere's a clause \u2014 old, written for the first crews who ever made planetfall somewhere and had to answer for themselves to whoever was already living there \u2014 that lets any one of us vouch for a contact's good faith, once, on our own standing. I'd never used mine. I used it just now, on the two of you, in a room with two people who'd have been perfectly happy to log tonight as a containment problem and be done with it. That's not a favor I did you. That's my entire credit with this ship, spent, and I'd rather not find out I spent it on a podcaster and a wedding videographer.\u201d",
  "They woke, or arrived, or simply were, back on the same stretch of Nevada highway the van had left from, eleven hours missing from a night neither of them had otherwise felt pass, and a story that was going to sound, Jesse said, exactly as insane told sober as it would have told drunk.",
  "They told Dana anyway.",
]));

// Chapter X
children.push(...chapterHeading("X", "The Priority"));
children.push(...guideQuote(
  "On difficult choices: there is almost always a second aircraft. Ask about the second aircraft.",
  "\u2014 The New Starter's Handbook, uncleared draft"
));
children.push(...body([
  "Jesse spent the drive back to cell service on the phone rather than asleep, and by the time the signal bars reappeared he had a name: Dr. Carl Daniels, the psychiatrist who had admitted the hitchhiker outside Elko and written him up, off the record, as the strangest case of an otherwise ordinary career. Daniels turned out to have the voice of a man who had spent thirty years being the calmest person in a room full of people having the worst day of their lives — slow, level, unhurried even over a bad phone connection — and agreed to talk on two conditions: never his hospital's name, and a sentence he said he'd been rehearsing for four years without ever finding the right audience for it. Patients who arrived with no papers, no dental records, and no language a linguistics department could place did not get profiled, or studied, or referred up any chain a genuinely unprecedented case should have climbed. They got a diagnosis nobody argued with, a locked bed, and, more often than the charts liked to admit, a fight they started for reasons nobody could later reconstruct, which either transferred them to a facility with better locks or got them killed by the staff who had to stop it. Daniels had watched some version of that happen four times in eleven years. He had stopped expecting anyone to call him about a fifth.",
  "Kade's son Milo was eleven, gangly in the specific way of a boy who has recently outgrown three pairs of shoes in one school year, with his father's same pale squint and none of his father's practice at hiding what he was feeling. He had spent six days in Denver General with a reaction to a medication whose fix could only be flown in from the one lab that made it, before a window closed that nobody outside a hospital would call dramatic and everybody inside one would.",
  "The same night, on the only aircraft cleared for both routes, Halo's field team had a narrow chance to bring in a Directorate asset named Anders Feld, swapped for a real infant forty-odd years ago and told the truth of it only six months back, in the flat, procedural way an organisation tells you anything it has been rehearsing for decades. He was sitting in a motel outside Reno with the closest thing on Earth to a native speaker's understanding of the ship, and he had decided, for reasons that were entirely his own, that he preferred being one person to being a delegate.",
  "The aircraft could not do both. It was, Dana would think later, the oldest shape in every one of the show's cousins on the shelf: a commander forced to choose between a child and a planet, and an audience trained by decades of old footage to expect that choosing the planet was both tragic and correct.",
  "Gladys, who had walked into the control room to deliver coffee and stayed because nobody told her to leave, asked the question nobody trained for this moment had thought to ask: \u201cWhy is it one plane? You've got helicopters in the lot upstairs. I saw them on the tour.\u201d",
  "It was not, in the end, a hard problem. It had only ever been hard inside a story that needed it to be. Kade authorized a second aircraft within ninety seconds of being asked a question his own staff had spent a career not asking, because they had all, without meaning to, been performing an old script instead of running the actual budget. The medication arrived. Feld arrived. Nobody had to become a commander all the way through that night. Somewhere in the same few hours a very different phone rang: Nora and Jesse, calling from a gas station outside Tonopah, talking over each other fast enough that Gladys had to ask them, twice, to slow down and start with the part about the van.",
  "What they described \u2014 the corridor, the Escort, a dozen hostile faces that had never learned a word of English, and a name, Feld's, handed to them before either of them had any reason to know it mattered \u2014 reached Dana within the hour, which meant she was holding Nora's account of a stranger's prediction in one hand and the stranger's actual arrival in the other before the night was through. She did not believe in omens. She was starting to believe in an organisation efficient enough to make omens look like paperwork.",
  "Feld, once he'd slept and eaten and been reassured four separate times that no one intended to put him in a drawer, was told, gently, that his name had reached two civilians on a ship he had never personally been aboard, before he himself had. He took this with less surprise than Dana expected. \u201cSomeone up there's been arguing my case longer than I have,\u201d was all he offered that night, and then, because it was apparently a reflex he could not switch off any more than Dominic could switch off his, corrected Warren's phrasing on a wholly unrelated point three sentences later \u2014 not activated, he said, triggered; a hybrid isn't a light switch, and if the Desk kept calling it one they'd keep being surprised by the dimmer \u2014 with the patient, slightly wearying precision of a man who had spent thirty years being the only translator in every room he'd ever stood in and had never once been allowed to let a wrong word pass uncorrected. He turned out to know something more useful than tactics regardless. The invasion plan, he explained, had been reapproved by the ship's ruling council eleven times in the last two centuries and amended on each occasion by delegates who would not be alive to see it carried out, which Feld said was the single most familiar thing about the whole story once you had sat through a Directorate budget meeting. Buried in that plan, in a clause nobody had ever had occasion to invoke, was a branch neither side had ever triggered, because triggering it required something the ship had never once been offered by anything it was planning to arrive on top of: an answer, freely given, instead of a broadcast simply taken.",
  "Halvorsen took the branch to the people above her, because that was the job, and the people above her heard an unused clause in a forty-thousand-year-old committee document and understood, with the particular clarity of officials who have never had to sit in a room with the thing they are describing, an opportunity: Starfall could carry a different kind of signal entirely, tuned not to answer the ship but to burn out every implant listening to it at once, in the one window in generations when every implant on the planet would be listening at the same second. Feld was the one who made them say what that meant in a sentence a civilian could hold: a receiver that size, driven that hard, does not politely switch off. It cooks. Every hybrid on Earth, Dominic included, would take that surge at the base of the skull in the same second, and nobody in the briefing room could tell Feld, when he asked twice, what percentage of them would only lose the implant and what percentage would lose rather more than that. A clean solution, on paper. Paper, Feld pointed out to a room that did not want to hear it twice, does not currently host anyone's brother-in-law.",
  "Dana believed him before she entirely wanted to. She had spent a career trusting a checklist over a feeling and had once put several thousand pieces of confetti into a military range because of it. She was no longer certain a colonel's confidence was a better instrument than forty-odd years of listening to the same committee from the inside.",
  "Buchanan called it caution dressed up as sentiment. Halvorsen, to her credit, did not overrule the plan and did not endorse it either, which left a gap in the chain of command exactly the width of one very fast wedding videographer.",
  "Priya, meanwhile, had been trying to turn Feld into data. Straight genetic sequencing came back, four separate times, defiantly normal \u2014 whatever had built him had apparently built him out of unmodified human material, which made a grim kind of sense once you'd heard Kade's explanation twice: why invent new biology when the old kind, in sufficient supply, works exactly as well and audits as human on every form that matters. What Feld's imaging did show, buried in a routine hospital X-ray taken for a broken wrist at age nine and exhumed from a records archive nobody remembered keeping, was a shadow the size of a grain of rice at the base of the skull, present in his file since childhood and never once flagged, because nobody reading a nine-year-old's wrist film in rural Ohio had any reason to go looking at the back of his neck. Priya ran the numbers with the grim satisfaction of a woman who had just solved exactly half a problem: the marker was real, cheap to find, and sitting on equipment every emergency room in the country already owned. It was also, as a public health measure, useless, because the only way to learn which of three hundred and thirty million Americans had one was to X-ray all three hundred and thirty million Americans, repeatedly, without any of them noticing they'd been asked \u2014 a sentence that survived contact with neither a budget nor a civil liberties lawyer. \u201cWe can prove it on one person,\u201d she told Dana. \u201cWe cannot prove it on a country. They know that. It's probably why they've never worried about the version of this where we just go looking.\u201d",
  "It was in the hour after, cleanup still ongoing, that Dana finally sat across a table, in person, from Nora Calder and Jesse Tran, in a Halo Systems break room that still smelled like the food court above it, and said the sentence Colonel Buchanan had been sitting on for two days: \u201cYour footage found Lissome before Brussels did, and the two of you found that facility before we'd found a use for you finding it. I'm sorry it took an actual abduction before anyone said thank you properly.\u201d",
  "Nora described the Escort again, in more detail than the gas-station call had allowed room for, and Feld, sitting in on the debrief because nobody had yet found a polite way to exclude him from a conversation about people exactly like him, went quiet in a way Dana hadn't seen from him before. \u201cI know what he meant,\u201d Feld said finally, \u201cabout having a choice put in front of him before I had mine. There's more than one of us who's asked that question. Not all. Enough that it matters. There's a woman on the council \u2014 Feld didn't have a name for her that meant anything in English, only a designation that translated, roughly and unhelpfully, to something like third seat, harvest committee \u2014 who's been voting against every hard-line proposal for eleven years running and losing every time by a smaller margin. She's not winning. She hasn't once won. But eleven years of losing by a shrinking margin is not nothing, and it's the reason the branch existed to be triggered at all.\u201d",
  "Nora, who had spent a month being told her camera lied, found she did not entirely know what to do with being believed. She asked, instead, the only question that had mattered to her since Thanksgiving: \u201cCan you fix my brother-in-law?\u201d",
  "Dana did not lie to her, which Nora would later say was the moment she decided to trust the whole strange building. \u201cI don't know yet. But we're not going to find out by keeping you in a diner in Nevada.\u201d",
]));

// Chapter XI
children.push(...chapterHeading("XI", "Biology"));
children.push(...guideQuote(
  "On fraternisation: not against the rules, strictly, since nobody has ever needed a rule for something nobody anticipated surviving long enough to attempt.",
  "\u2014 The New Starter's Handbook, uncleared draft"
));
children.push(...body([
  "The break room had a vending machine that dispensed only the bottom row, a mini-fridge hosting someone else's forgotten sandwich, and, at that hour, not one single other person who needed anything from either of them. Nora had spent the last ten minutes silently rehearsing every professional reason to say goodnight and go find a motel of her own. She had not moved from the spot by the counter, and had stopped pretending, even to herself, that she was going to.",
  "Jesse, standing close enough that his shoulder had apparently decided to become permanent architecture near hers, finally said the thing he'd been rehearsing since the motel outside Reno, and picked possibly the worst-lit room in the state to say it in. It was not, by his own later account, a good speech — he'd had four days to write it and had spent all four of them on the wrong draft — but it had the one virtue a speech like that actually needs, which is that he meant every word of it and stopped talking while that was still true. Nora told him afterward that this was, in fact, exactly the right amount of romance for the year she was having, and that she'd have been suspicious of anything with better lighting.",
  "She didn't answer him with words. For a woman who had spent eleven years professionally certain that a picture told the truth better than a speech, it was the most honest thing she'd done all week. She closed the distance his shoulder had already been arguing didn't exist and kissed him the way you kiss someone after you have both nearly died more than once in the same two days and have run out of patience for finding out later. He kissed her back like a man who had been rehearsing the wrong version of this the whole time and had only just found the right one.",
  "They surfaced from it just long enough for Nora to say, with the particular precision of a woman narrating her own bad decisions as she made them, “You mean we should let the psychology take over. Talk it through properly. Like adults.” Jesse considered this for exactly as long as it took him to shake his head. “No,” he said. “Biology. It's much more fun.” Nora did not have a rebuttal ready, which she maintained afterward was rather the point of biology in the first place.",
  "They did not go back to separate motel rooms that night, and this book does not intend to follow them through the door beyond noting that neither of them thought once about Nevada, the ship, or Starfall for as long as the two of them could manage to keep the world that small. Nora would say, much later, that it was the first night in a month she had stopped being afraid in full sentences. Jesse would say less about it than that, because Jesse, for once in his life, had run out of things to narrate.",
  "Word travels fast in a building with three occupants and one working elevator. Warren found out first, from the two coffees Jesse ordered the next morning instead of one, and said nothing about it, which was, from Warren, roughly the civic equivalent of throwing a parade. Priya found out second, deduced it from twelve feet away in under four seconds flat, and immediately opened a betting pool she declined to show either of them the terms of. Gladys, who had privately known since the parking lot outside Lissome and had simply been waiting for the paperwork to catch up with the feeling, said only, “Well. About time,” and went back to the tip line, entirely satisfied with how the universe was running that particular week.",
  "Steve, informed of none of this and caring less, ate a second houseplant three states away and was declared, by unanimous and entirely unscientific consensus, to be thriving.",
]));

// Chapter XII
children.push(...chapterHeading("XII", "Starfall"));
children.push(...guideQuote(
  "On endings: the species will not be told. It will simply notice, eventually, that the sky feels less watched, and assume this is progress.",
  "\u2014 The New Starter's Handbook, uncleared draft"
));
children.push(...body([
  "The Desk drove down together, all five of them and the alpaca in one van that smelled, by hour six, unmistakably of alpaca, and spent the last quiet stretch of highway before Texas doing the thing they had never once done in the months since Lissome, which was nothing in particular: Gladys read the paper editions of two newspapers she still had delivered on principle, Warren dozed with a folder open on his lap he had no intention of reading, Priya argued with Jesse about a podcast episode neither of them would remember by morning, and Dana drove, watching all of it in the mirror the way a woman watches something she has decided, without quite announcing it to herself, to keep. Nobody mentioned Starfall for a hundred and forty miles. It was, Dana thought, the closest the Desk had ever come to being an actual desk, as opposed to a basement full of people who happened to work near each other.",
  "Starfall went ahead because canceling it would have cost Halo Systems more market confidence than the eclipse itself was worth, and because Kade decided, after a conversation with Dana and Halvorsen that took place mostly in a stairwell neither of them trusted the building's microphones enough to skip, that the safest place to fight a broadcast was inside it.",
  "The event filled a stadium in Texas — eighty thousand seats, the heat still coming up off the concourse concrete after sundown, popcorn and sunscreen and hot asphalt in every breath — and a billion screens beyond: a constellation of satellites unfurling reflective sails to paint a second ring of light across a natural eclipse, timed to music, hosted by a Halo Systems executive whose smile Trip Calloway — mid-forties, silver-templed, a voice trained to sound sincere on command — had personally rehearsed for eleven minutes while explaining, without quite explaining, why the broadcast could not run one second long. It was, by simple satellite count, the single largest coordinated act any orbital constellation on Earth had ever performed, a fact three separate press releases mentioned and none of them explained the actual reason for.",
  "Warren worked the stadium crowd with a printed list cross-referenced from a wedding guest list, a Nevada facility's badge logs, and three motel receipts, because he did not believe in throwing anything away and, that day, seventy years of the habit finally paid its debt at once. Gladys ran a tip line that was, for one night, not a cell phone in a drawer but four hundred volunteers Jesse's podcast audience had organized in nine hours flat, once Jesse finally, on air, said the true thing instead of the scary thing: if you see someone in this crowd who doesn't flinch, call this number, don't confront them, just call. Somewhere in the middle of that volunteer roster, relaying calls from three counties over on a shortwave set he had been perfecting since a decade before anyone believed him, sat Gary Huff, running the single most useful hour of his civil-defence career, and never once finding out that it was.",
  "Priya sat in the control trailer with Halo's engineers and, more quietly, with Anders Feld, building the thing Colonel Buchanan's office had authorized and did not yet know was about to be replaced: not the overload signal sealed in a briefing folder upstairs, but an answer, assembled from the one branch of the ship's own forty-thousand-year-old plan that nobody had ever had occasion to use, structured as a question it had always technically allowed for.",
  "Buchanan's authorization for the overload arrived with forty minutes to spare and a signature that made it, on paper, the only sanctioned plan in the building. It was Jesse who said the thing plainly, in a trailer full of people with better clearances than his: that a plan which required not asking Dominic Reyes, or the several hundred thousand people quietly like him in that stadium alone, was not a peace plan whatever the folder called it, and that four hundred podcast listeners were currently standing by with a phone number precisely because ordinary people had gotten tired of being managed by folders. Nobody in the trailer outranked a man who had already found the facility Buchanan's own field team had been a day late to.",
  "Kade read the authorization once, looked at Feld, looked at Dana, and did the thing his own file would later describe, admiringly, as a command decision, and everyone actually present would describe, less formally, as a man deciding that a signature from Brussels did not outrank someone who had actually met the thing they were about to overload. He told his engineers to run the answer Priya and Feld had built instead. He did not call Brussels first. There would be time to be reprimanded for it later, assuming there was a later. It was the second time in two weeks he'd looked at an authorized, tragic-shaped plan and asked, out loud, why it had to be the only aircraft.",
  "Nora found Dominic in section 114, three rows from where Mabs sat wondering why her husband had insisted, uncharacteristically, on seats that faced the broadcast screen instead of the eclipse itself. Steve, three feet away in a service corridor he had no business occupying, would not walk past a sound technician whose credentials were perfect. The technician, feeling an alpaca's opinion of him with an accuracy that should have worried him sooner, broke for the master feed the moment Warren's radio crackled with a Dipstick color nobody wanted to read aloud, and got two switches thrown before Warren — seventy years of filing habits notwithstanding a body that could still, on the right afternoon, cover forty yards of stadium corridor — put him on the ground a stride short of the third. That was the whole physical contest the climax of this book had to offer, and this book is not going to apologize for how short it was. Wars are rarely won on the last switch. They are won, more often, on whether anyone was standing near it.",
  "The ship's broadcast woke into Starfall exactly the way Kade had feared: reaching, through a frequency layered under a light show a billion people had chosen to watch on purpose, for every sleeping hybrid in range at once, the way it had reached for them one at a time, quietly, for forty-six patient years. Priya's filter caught it the same way any technosignature search catches anything: not by understanding the signal first, but by noticing that the noise floor had stopped behaving like noise. Nine seconds after the pattern changed shape, she had a spike nobody could argue with. What went out over the retuned signal was not a jam and was not the overload Brussels had signed off on. It was offered rather than aimed, and structured, for the first time in forty-six years, as a question the ship's own plan had always technically allowed for and had never once been given cause to use. Something on the order of a billion invitations arrived, and one, freely made, got a reply.",
  "Feld heard it before Priya's screens confirmed anything: a particular shape folded into that reply, a small stubborn asymmetry in an otherwise clean signal that he recognized the way you recognize a friend's handwriting on an envelope you weren't expecting. He didn't explain it to the trailer, and nobody in the trailer was positioned to ask him to. He only said, quietly, to no one in particular, that somebody up there had argued for exactly this shape and won, for once, and that he hoped whoever it was still had the standing left to keep arguing tomorrow.",
  "Nora reached Dominic before the reply finished. She did not have a plan beyond her sister's hand in hers, a camera she'd left, for once, in the car, and eleven years of watching a face for the fraction of a second before it decided what it was about to feel — the same fraction of a second she'd caught on video the first night, and had spent a month wishing she'd been wrong about. That, more than anything Kade's engineers had built, was the instrument she trusted to tell her when to speak. She said his name, and Mabs's, and the fishing trip Curtis had never finished describing, ordinary words with no procedure behind them at all. It was the same lesson Feld had spent an hour trying to explain to a trailer full of colonels: the ship had never once been spoken to as if it might listen. Neither, really, had Dominic.",
  "What she got, when it dropped, was her brother-in-law blinking hard, twice, the way a person blinks who has been holding very still for longer than he realized, and asking Mabs, with real confusion, why she was crying, which was as close to an answer as Kade had ever managed to give Dana about the alternative.",
  "Above all of them the eclipse reached totality on schedule, and the satellites did the one part of their job the public had actually paid to watch: a second ring of light closing slow and silver around the moon's dark disc, drawn across eighty thousand upturned faces in a Texas stadium and a billion screens beyond it, while a species that had almost changed hands twenty minutes earlier filmed the sky on its phones and called it beautiful, which, separately from everything else that had just happened to it, it also was.",
  "Nobody could prove, afterward, that they'd stopped an invasion instead of a very expensive light show having a technical glitch, and nobody in Brussels was ever going to be told that the authorized signal had been quietly swapped out by a satellite executive on the say-so of a podcaster and a defector. That was, Kade told Dana on the field afterward, watching a billion-dollar broadcast fold itself back into cargo cases, the whole nature of the job now. You do not get a press conference, and you do not get a surrender, because there was never anyone in command over there who had ever been asked the right question before. You get a forty-thousand-year-old ship that, for the first time in the forty-six years it's been watching us, stood down a scheduled action because someone finally answered it on purpose instead of aiming something at it. Officially, Buchanan's plan worked exactly as authorized. You write the truth down, in a file three people are allowed to read, and you go home, and you let the folder win the argument it never actually had.",
]));

// Denouement paragraph before afterword (short closing beat)
children.push(
  P("\u2217", { align: AlignmentType.CENTER, size: 24, spacingAfter: 200, spacingBefore: 300 }),
  ...body([
    "The Desk did not close. It acquired, in the following months, a coffee maker that worked, and a standing invitation for a podcaster and a wedding videographer to stop by whenever either of them found something the internet had tried too hard to make disappear. Mabs and Dominic are, as of this printing, still married, and have agreed, as a couple, never to discuss section 114 again, which Nora considers the single greatest act of diplomacy either of them will ever perform.",
    "Jesse's podcast added a co-host partway through the following season, which several listeners found suspicious and correct in roughly equal measure, and which Nora still refuses to discuss on the record, on the grounds that some evidence should simply be allowed to speak for itself. Gary Huff, back in Lissome, still maintains he called it, and \u2014 in the scattered, deniable way this book's cousins have all learned to allow such men \u2014 he sort of did.",
    "Milo made a full recovery, went back to school, and was, as far as anyone at Halo Systems could determine, the only person involved in the entire Starfall operation who never once learned how close a badly timed flight schedule had come to being the whole story. Elena Marsh, who ran the desk two floors above Dana's for the length of this book without once being told what either desk was actually for, finally found out in a Directorate debrief three weeks after Starfall, went very pale, and sent Dana a memo the next morning signed, for the first time in either of their careers, with a first name and nothing official above it. They have had exactly one dinner since. Neither of them has called it that out loud yet. And \u201cthe Chen index,\u201d after an internal memo Halvorsen personally signed, is now, on every document that matters, the Priya Chen index, a correction Priya still brings up at least once a week, entirely on principle, to people who have long since stopped needing to hear it.",
    "Priya's own numbers, when she finally had the week to run them properly, would not stay as clean as the night itself had felt. The retuned signal reached every implant within range of the broadcast, which turned out not to be quite the same thing as reaching every implant on Earth; a rural hospital outside Brandon, Manitoba, logged one admission four days after Starfall that matched the old pattern almost exactly — no papers, no dental records, a fight started over nothing anyone could later reconstruct — too far from any tower the retune had actually touched. Priya kept that file open longer than any of the others on her desk. She has not closed it since, and has stopped promising anyone, including herself, that she will.",
    "Halvorsen's committee still meets three blocks from NATO headquarters, under a sign that still says LOGISTICS. Colonel Buchanan's report on Starfall remains, on paper, entirely correct, a fact she has not once let anyone in that building forget; if anything, the night convinced her more thoroughly than a decade of Halvorsen's patience ever managed, that the only reason the diplomacy got its chance at all was that a loaded alternative was sitting right behind it, and that the two are not, in her arithmetic, separable. Nobody has found a way to prove her wrong that doesn't also require explaining what actually happened, which nobody currently alive with the clearance to explain it is prepared to do. She is, as of this printing, drafting a considerably larger version of the same authorization, for a considerably larger broadcast two years out, and has not yet been told no.",
    "The ship did not go away, and it did not stay silent either; Feld, who stayed on as the only translator either side actually trusted, reported that the branch they had triggered was still open, and that more of the council than anyone had expected seemed inclined to keep the conversation going rather than finish what their great-great-grandparents had started. Of the Escort himself Feld had less to report, and said it more slowly than he said anything else that year: the council's response to a hybrid spending his one lifetime's vouching-right on two Americans in a parking lot had not been recorded anywhere Feld was cleared to read, and nobody who might know was answering the question, in either direction. Nora asked him to keep asking. He said he would. Some debts, this book already admitted once, do not close just because somebody on the outside would like them to.",
    "Nothing in this business ever fully resolves; that was the one lesson every cousin on the shelf had already tried to teach, and this book saw no reason to lie about it now. But it lost a broadcast it had waited forty-six years to use, and patience, Dana had learned from watching a rocket of her own design put confetti into the wrong part of the sky, is a resource you can run out of even after forty thousand years of it.",
    "Dana went looking for Nowak twice more that year, out of an itch she never quite named out loud. The Ibex Aerospace settlement files listed him. No government directory did, and had not, as far as three separate agencies could determine, for as long as any of them kept records. Nobody she asked had ever met him in a room. On slow afternoons she found herself replaying the one phone call that had put her in that basement in the first place, and wondering, without ever quite finishing the thought, because finishing it changed too much about the year that followed, whether somebody considerably more patient than a personnel office had wanted an out-of-work rocket engineer specifically underneath that fluorescent hum, in that basement, months before anyone official had a reason to need her there. She never got an answer. Eventually, she stopped asking out loud, which is not, this book would like to note for the record, the same thing as having stopped wondering.",
  ])
);

/* ---------------- AFTERWORD ---------------- */
children.push(
  pageBreak(),
  headline("A NOTE ON THE COUSINS"),
  ...body([
    "Protocol Flamingo is not a retelling. It is not based on any film, television series, or declassified program, and not on any office of any real government, including the ones this book borrows its shrug from \u2014 the United States' All-domain Anomaly Resolution Office, and the real allied liaisons that really do work three blocks from real NATO headquarters on subjects that have nothing to do with any of this. Nora Calder, Dana Osei, the Joint Sky Directorate, Halo Systems, the Feed, and Steve are all invented, along with their budgets, their coffee makers, and their confetti. Every name in this book was checked against all nine of its cousins \u2014 OVNI(s), The Invaders, UFO, Dark Skies, the 1953 and 2005 Wars of the Worlds, Independence Day, Enemy Mine, and They Live \u2014 and belongs to none of them.",
    "The debt, though, is real, and it runs in two directions. From the quiet cousins this book took its central shape: an invasion nobody official will admit to, fought as two stories at once \u2014 a private fight, from The Invaders and Dark Skies, and a government effort, from OVNI(s) and UFO, whose commander answered to an international body and rarely got to be anything else. Where those loud, public cousins let the sky announce itself to everyone at once \u2014 the tripods under the street, the discs over every capital a global audience could name, the two soldiers stranded with no government watching at all \u2014 this book kept faith with its quieter relatives instead. The wedding guests in Lissome still think an alpaca was the strangest thing that happened that Saturday, and nobody involved intends to correct them.",
    "It still borrowed a few tricks from the loud side of the family: a name recited instead of a man erased, in the oldest tradition Enemy Mine has to offer; a countdown hidden in noise, the way a certain holiday picture hid one in static; a cellar prepper convinced he alone saw it coming, in the spirit of a man with a slit in a farmhouse wall; a white flag, offered once, in good faith, and remembered for how briefly it lasted; and, from They Live, without any eyewear at all, the suspicion that the occupation had already incorporated and was running its numbers through the very broadcasts we volunteer to watch.",
    "It also ended up making an argument neither OVNI(s) nor UFO quite needed to make, because their commanders were mostly right: sometimes the government effort is not the adult in the room. This book's Directorate is well-staffed, well-funded, and, for about forty stressful minutes near the end, working from an authorized plan that would have solved the wrong problem, loudly, over the objection of the one person who had actually met the thing it wanted to solve. The correction came from a wedding videographer, a podcaster, a satellite executive who stopped checking with Brussels, and a translator who used to be the thing everyone was afraid of. The ship, once anyone actually sat still and listened to it, turned out to be less a villain than a family that had been travelling so long it had forgotten it was allowed to stop and ask \u2014 not entirely wrong, from where it was standing, to want a new home after forty thousand years without one, and wrong only about the method, and about whether anyone would ever offer it a better way to ask. That felt like the honest ending for a book that spent this much time insisting ordinary witnesses deserve to be believed, and occasionally deserve to be listened to before the people with the clearances are.",
    "If the tone leans a little British and a little absurd \u2014 committees that never adjourn, handbooks nobody clears, an alpaca with better security clearance than a technical consultant \u2014 that is a deliberate tip of the hat to Douglas Adams, who taught a couple of generations that the end of the world is always, somewhere, someone's Tuesday paperwork. Nothing here is drawn from his books; the debt is to his tone, not his text.",
    "Nine cousins stood behind real programs and told their stories again without borrowing a line of dialogue. This one stands behind all nine at once, borrows nothing but their shape, and runs its two halves \u2014 the private fight and the government effort, and the two people who end up doing both at once, and occasionally doing the government's job better than it did \u2014 until they finally sit down in the same diner. If you've read the others, you already know the sky in this book. If you haven't, you can start here. Brussels will still be arguing about the minutes either way.",
  ]),
  P("Look up anyway. Nobody else is going to mention it.", { italics: true, align: AlignmentType.CENTER, size: 24, spacingBefore: 300, spacingAfter: 100 }),
);

